  t["Resolvemos $f'(x) = 0$: las raíces son $x = "] = "We solve $f'(x) = 0$: the roots are $x = ";
  t["Resolver $-3x + 7 \\le 1$."] = "Solve $-3x + 7 \\le 1$.";
  t["Resolver $2x^2 - 5x - 3 = 0$."] = "Solve $2x^2 - 5x - 3 = 0$.";
  t["Resolver $3^{2x} - 4\\cdot 3^x + 3 = 0$."] = "Solve $3^{2x} - 4\\cdot 3^x + 3 = 0$.";
  t["Resolver $AX = B$ es lo que hace un programa cuando ajusta el color de una imagen, corrige la perspectiva de una foto de un documento o calcula cómo se reparten las fuerzas en un puente. Y las potencias de una matriz son la forma de ver el futuro de un sistema que evoluciona paso a paso: si $A$ dice cómo cambia una población de un año al siguiente, $A^{50}$ dice cómo estará dentro de cincuenta años. Es la idea que desarrolla el tema de [[av-markov|cadenas de Markov]]."] = "Solving $AX = B$ is what a program does when it adjusts the colour of an image, corrects the perspective of a photo of a document or works out how forces are shared in a bridge. And the powers of a matrix are the way to see the future of a system that evolves step by step: if $A$ says how a population changes from one year to the next, $A^{50}$ says how it will be in fifty years. It is the idea developed in the topic on [[av-markov|Markov chains]].";
  t["Resolver $XA = B$ con $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ y $B = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\end{pmatrix}$."] = "Solve $XA = B$ with $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\end{pmatrix}$.";
  t["Resolver $\\begin{cases} 3x + 2y = 12 \\\\ 2x - 3y = -5 \\end{cases}$"] = "Solve $\\begin{cases} 3x + 2y = 12 \\\\ 2x - 3y = -5 \\end{cases}$";
  t["Resolver $\\begin{cases} x + y + z = 6 \\\\ 2x - y + z = 3 \\\\ x + 2y - z = 2 \\end{cases}$"] = "Solve $\\begin{cases} x + y + z = 6 \\\\ 2x - y + z = 3 \\\\ x + 2y - z = 2 \\end{cases}$";
  t["Resolver $\\dfrac{x+1}{2} - \\dfrac{x-2}{3} = 1$."] = "Solve $\\dfrac{x+1}{2} - \\dfrac{x-2}{3} = 1$.";
  t["Resolver $\\operatorname{sen} x = \\frac{1}{2}$ no es como resolver $2x = 1$. Aquí hay <strong>infinitas soluciones</strong>, porque las funciones trigonométricas son periódicas."] = "Solving $\\sin x = \\frac{1}{2}$ is not like solving $2x = 1$. Here there are <strong>infinitely many solutions</strong>, because the trigonometric functions are periodic.";
  t["Resolver completas e incompletas"] = "Solving complete and incomplete ones";
